  "query_preview": "..A dry westerly zonal flow will remain over Arizona\nthrough Thursday before a Pacific trough delivers unsettle \nweather over the weekend.\n\nFor today through Thursday...With zonal flow aloft fair weather \nis forecast, with mild temperatures and dry conditions. As the \nend of the week trough approaches Arizona, look for increasing \nmid- to high-level clouds from Wednesday into Thursday. As far as \nthe winds, a very weak shortwave trough brushing Arizona on \nTuesday will deliver southwest winds a 10-20 mph during the day. \nLighter winds on Wednesday behind the weak shortwave will be \nfollowed by stronger southwest winds on Thursday as the more \nsubstantial weekend trough approaches.\n\nFrom Friday into early Monday...A strong Pacific trough will move\nacross the western states tapping into subtropical moisture. \nFriday will see the first chance for precipitation as a shortwave \ntrough interacts with increasing moisture to produce scattered \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms. We are expecting the Friday \nrain event to remain on the light side with liquid equivalent \namounts ranging from 0.05 to 0.40 inches. Severe thunderstorms \nare no expected. Moisture will continue to increase on Saturday \nbut with no large-scale upward forcing the main source of lift \nwill be in the form orographic assent where .05 to 0.15 inches of \nprecipitation are expected over the mountain areas of northern \nArizona. There could be a few drips Halloween night so plan\naccordingly. The Friday/Saturday system will be relatively warm \nwith any accumulating snow likely confined to above 9000 feet. The\nmain body of the Pacific trough will move through from Sunday \ninto Monday. Strong large scale lift, moderate upslope flow and \nsubtropical moisture will combine for widespread showers and \nisolated thunderstorms. At the current time we are looking at \nrainfall totals ranging from 0.50-1.50 inches with the second, \nstronger trough, but this is apt to change as forecast amounts \ncould go higher with subtropical moisture in play. The air mass \nwill remain mild with accumulating snow levels remaining largely \nabove 9000 feet. \n\nLater next Monday and for most of next week...Fair and dry \nweather returns under high pressure.",
